@page "/"
@page "/modes"
@implements IDisposable
@inject AreaSnapshotCache Cache
@inject ActivityLog Log
@inject LightingEngineHost Engine
@inject ModeService Service
@inject HaCatalog Catalog
@inject ILogger<Dashboard> Logger

@*
	The board — the home surface. The visitor arrives with a room and a time in mind ("why didn't the terrace
	light come on around 23:00?"), so the page is a timeline: the schedule band across the top, one lane per
	room beneath it, a now-line through everything, and the decision log underneath in words.

	Three rules do the work, and all three live in BoardView so nothing here can disagree with a test:

	THE DARK COCKPIT. A room behaving normally says nothing. Its lane is an empty track, and past
	BoardView.LaneBudget rooms it stops claiming a row at all and becomes a chip on the quiet shelf. Seventeen
	rooms are scannable in one second because fourteen of them are not asking to be read. Below the budget
	nothing folds: in a two-room house an empty track IS the answer, and a board with two lanes hidden behind a
	footnote would look broken rather than quiet.

	EXCEPTIONS ARE HOISTED. The warning dim and the three ways somebody else's decision is standing are lifted
	into the tray above the board, each one line naming what happens and when it ends. What is left over is the
	entire reassurance budget this design allows: one sentence saying the rest are doing what the schedule says.

	FLOOR HEADERS EARN THEIR SPACE. This house has six of them. A floor is headed only when it has a lane to
	head, so the headers appear as rooms get busy rather than sitting there dividing nothing. FloorGrouping owns
	the grouping and its degradation rule — a house with no floors gets no headers at all — and is not
	reimplemented here.

	THIS PAGE IS READ-ONLY ABOUT CONFIGURATION. The master switch and the house-mode selector still command Home
	Assistant through ModeService, exactly as before; they change entities, not the document. Nothing else here
	writes anything, which is what lets the board ship beside a settings rework.

	The /modes route is kept so old bookmarks land here.
*@

<PageTitle>Dashboard &mdash; Adaptive lighting</PageTitle>

@*
	The house bar: the one control that stops everything, the mode the house is in, and who is home — one strip
	where there used to be a card and then two more.

	This is the board's chrome, and chrome is measured against what it displaces. Stacked, the three cost about
	three hundred pixels before the first lane — on a phone, the whole first screen — to say three short facts
	and offer two controls. Side by side in one panel they cost roughly a third of that and say the same things,
	because almost all of the height was a second line under each fact restating what the fact already said.

	What survives whole is the master switch: it is leftmost, it keeps the state colour on its edge and its own
	large On/Off button, and it keeps a line under it whenever there is news — paused, or unreachable. Only the
	"everything is fine" line went, into the button's own hover, because a permanent sentence saying nothing is
	wrong is exactly the reassurance budget this design refuses to spend. The mode's meaning line went the same
	way and to a better home: the House tab now says in full what each mode does.

	All three read on the same ticker; only the master switch and the mode act, and both through ModeService's
	existing config-resolved calls. Nothing here touches the document.
*@
<div class="housebar">
	@if (_masterSwitch is { } master)
	{
		<div class="housebar-cell housebar-master @MasterClass(master)">
			<span class="housebar-lab">Adaptive lighting</span>
			<div class="housebar-main">
				<span class="housebar-value">@MasterStateWord(master)</span>
				<button type="button"
						class="toggle @(master is { IsAvailable: true, AdaptiveLightingOn: true } ? "on" : "")"
						disabled="@(!master.IsAvailable || !master.Toggle.CanToggle)"
						title="@MasterTitle(master)"
						@onclick="ToggleMaster">
					@(master.IsAvailable ? master.AdaptiveLightingOn ? "On" : "Off" : "?")
				</button>
			</div>
			@if (MasterNote(master) is { Length: > 0 } note)
			{
				<p class="housebar-note">@note</p>
			}
		</div>
	}

	<HouseModePanel HouseMode="_houseMode" Message="@_modeMessage" Confirmation="@_modeConfirmation" OnSelect="SelectMode" />
	<WhoIsHomePanel People="_people" />
</div>

@if (_snapshots.Any(area => area.KillSwitchActive))
{
	<div class="notice bad">
		<strong>Adaptive lighting is paused.</strong> Nothing was turned off, but no lights will change until you
		turn it back on above.
	</div>
}
else if (!Engine.IsRunning && Cache.HasData)
{
	<div class="notice bad">
		<strong>Adaptive lighting has stopped@(Engine.Fault is { Length: > 0 } ? ":" : ".")</strong>
		@Engine.Fault
		The timeline below shows what each room was doing when it stopped.
	</div>
}

@*
	The empty states, in the order they take precedence. A broken connection and a stopped engine keep the
	conditions and the words they always had and are answered first: neither may be dressed up as onboarding,
	because "choose which rooms to switch on" would send somebody to the settings page to fix a problem that
	is not there.
*@
@if (!Cache.HasData && !Engine.IsAttached)
{
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>Connecting to Home Assistant&hellip;</strong>
		<p>This is normal for a minute after a restart. If it doesn't clear, the connection is down.</p>
	</div>
}
else if (!Cache.HasData && !Engine.IsRunning)
{
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>Connected, but adaptive lighting isn't running.</strong>
		<p>@(Engine.Fault ?? "No reason was recorded.")</p>
		<p>See <a href="config">Configuration</a> for details.</p>
	</div>
}
else if (AreaView.IsAwaitingRoomChoice(_rooms, Engine.IsAttached))
{
	@*
		The first-run state, and the one page a new install lands on: set-up has found the rooms and written
		them all switched off, so the system is finished with its half and waiting for the owner's. It is not
		an error and is deliberately not styled as one — no warning edge, no fault colour, one thing to do.

		It also covers a house whose owner switched every room off later, which is the same sentence and the
		same next step.
	*@
	<div class="onboarding">
		<p class="onboarding-lead">@FoundLine</p>
		<ul class="room-chips">
			@foreach (RoomView room in _rooms)
			{
				<li class="room-chip">
					<span class="room-chip-name">@room.Name</span>
					@* No count when discovery has not answered: "(0 lights)" would read as a fault in a room that has none. *@
					@if (room.LightCount > 0)
					{
						<span class="room-chip-count">@LightsWord(room.LightCount)</span>
					}
				</li>
			}
		</ul>
		<a class="onboarding-action" href="config?section=areas">Choose which rooms to switch on</a>
	</div>
}
else if (!Cache.HasData)
{
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>Starting up &mdash; waiting for the first report from your rooms.</strong>
		<p>This normally takes a few seconds.</p>
	</div>
}
else if (_lanes.Count == 0)
{
	@*
		Reports have arrived, but none from a room that is switched on — a state filtering makes possible and an
		unfiltered dashboard never could. Said plainly rather than left as an axis over nothing.
	*@
	<div class="empty">
		<strong>None of the rooms you switched on has reported yet.</strong>
		<p>Each one gets a lane here as it checks in.</p>
	</div>
}
else
{
	@*
		The exception tray. Each chip is a room worth a look, said as one line, and the whole tray is a link
		surface: a chip goes to the room's own page. The closing sentence is the only thing this design says
		about the rooms that are fine.
	*@
	<div class="tray">
		@foreach (AreaSnapshot area in _exceptions)
		{
			<a class="tray-chip @TrayEdge(area.State)" href="@RoomHref(area.AreaId)">
				<StateChip State="area.State" Kelvin="area.ColorTempKelvin" Bare="true" />
				<span class="tray-text"><b>@area.AreaName</b> &mdash; @BoardView.ExceptionLine(area, _now)</span>
			</a>
		}
		<span class="tray-quiet">@BoardView.QuietRoomsLine(_lanes.Count, _exceptions.Count)</span>
	</div>

	@* The board's own mark. i-lanes exists precisely for this heading: ui-design-c §6.2 refused both drawn
	   dashboard glyphs — a tile grid and a gauge are the wrong metaphor for a surface that is a timeline —
	   and commissioned three unequal tracks cut by a now-line instead, which is this board in miniature. It
	   is the only heading on the page, so nothing repeats it. *@
	<section class="board">
		<div class="board-head">
			<h2 class="board-title"><Icon Name="@Glyph.Lanes" Class="ic-head" />Timeline</h2>
			<span class="board-span">@BoardView.Clock(_window.Start) &rarr; @BoardView.Clock(_window.End)</span>
			<span class="board-since">@SinceLine</span>
		</div>

		@* The scroller. On a phone the tracks move sideways under a name column that stays put; the axis, the
		   band and every lane share one grid so a block, its hour and its period line up. *@
		<div class="board-scroll">
			<div class="board-grid" style="--hour-w: @Pct(100.0 / _window.Hours)%">
				<div class="board-axis">
					<div class="board-gutter"></div>
					<div class="board-track board-track-bare">
						@* An hour the now-tag is sitting on is not drawn: two times overlapping in one place is
						   worse than one, and of the two the live one wins. *@
						@for (int index = 0; index < _ticks.Count; index++)
						{
							double at = index * 100.0 / _window.Hours;

							@if (Math.Abs(at - _nowPercent) >= 3.5)
							{
								<span class="board-tick @TickClass(index)" style="left: @Pct(at)%">
									@_ticks[index].ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)
								</span>
							}
						}
					</div>
				</div>

				@* The schedule band: what the house was told to do, above what each room actually did. *@
				<div class="board-band">
					<div class="board-gutter"></div>
					<div class="board-track board-track-bare">
						@if (_band.Count == 0)
						{
							<span class="board-band-none">no schedule could be placed</span>
						}
						@foreach (BandSegment segment in _band)
						{
							<span class="band-seg"
								  style="left: @Pct(segment.LeftPct)%; width: @Pct(segment.WidthPct)%; background: @KelvinColour.Css(segment.Kelvin)"
								  title="@($"{segment.Name} — {segment.Kelvin} K")"></span>

							@if (BoardView.IsLabelled(segment))
							{
								<span class="band-name" style="left: @Pct(segment.LeftPct)%">@segment.Name</span>
							}
						}
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="board-lanes">
					@foreach (FloorGroup<BoardLane> group in _groups)
					{
						IReadOnlyList<BoardLane> busy = [.. group.Items.Where(lane => !_quiet.Contains(lane.Key))];

						@* A floor is headed only when it has a lane to head. Six floors and three busy rooms
						   would otherwise be six separators dividing almost nothing. *@
						@if (busy.Count > 0 && AreaView.ShowsHeader(_groups.Count, group.Floor))
						{
							<div class="board-floor">
								<span class="board-floor-name">@AreaView.FloorTitle(group.Floor)</span>
							</div>
						}

						@foreach (BoardLane lane in busy)
						{
							<a class="lane" href="@RoomHref(lane.AreaId)" title="@LaneTitle(lane)" @key="lane.Key">
								<span class="lane-name">
									<StateChip State="lane.Latest.State" Kelvin="lane.Latest.ColorTempKelvin" Bare="true" />
									<span class="lane-room">@lane.Name</span>
								</span>
								<span class="board-track">
									@foreach (LaneBlock block in lane.Blocks)
									{
										<span class="blk blk-@block.Kind.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()"
											  style="left: @Pct(block.LeftPct)%; width: @Pct(block.WidthPct)%@(block.Kelvin is { } kelvin ? $"; --blk: {KelvinColour.Css(kelvin)}" : null)"></span>
									}
									@if (lane.Next is { } next)
									{
										<span class="lane-mark" style="left: @Pct(next.LeftPct)%"><i>@next.Label</i></span>
									}
								</span>
							</a>
						}
					}
				</div>

				@* One now-line through the axis, the band and every lane. Offset past the sticky name column so
				   it stands where the tracks actually begin. *@
				<span class="board-now" style="left: @NowOffset"></span>
				<span class="board-now-tag" style="left: @NowOffset">@BoardView.Clock(_now)</span>
			</div>
		</div>

		@*
			The quiet shelf. Rooms with nothing to show, as chips rather than as empty rows — the same rooms,
			the same tap through to their pages, a fifth of the height. Empty, and absent, whenever every room
			kept its lane.
		*@
		@if (_quiet.Count > 0)
		{
			<div class="board-quiet">
				<span class="board-quiet-lab">Quiet since @BoardView.Clock(_window.Start)</span>
				@foreach (FloorGroup<BoardLane> group in _groups)
				{
					IReadOnlyList<BoardLane> resting = [.. group.Items.Where(lane => _quiet.Contains(lane.Key))];

					@if (resting.Count > 0)
					{
						@if (AreaView.ShowsHeader(_groups.Count, group.Floor))
						{
							<span class="board-quiet-floor">@AreaView.FloorTitle(group.Floor)</span>
						}

						@foreach (BoardLane lane in resting)
						{
							<a class="quiet-chip" href="@RoomHref(lane.AreaId)" title="@LaneTitle(lane)" @key="lane.Key">
								<StateChip State="lane.Latest.State" Kelvin="lane.Latest.ColorTempKelvin" Bare="true" />
								@lane.Name
							</a>
						}
					}
				}
			</div>
		}

		@* No history at all. The lanes are honestly empty, and saying why beats leaving somebody to conclude
		   the board is broken. *@
		@if (_entries.Count == 0)
		{
			<p class="board-note">
				Nothing has been recorded yet. Recording starts when adaptive lighting starts, and a room appears
				the first time it decides something.
			</p>
		}

		@*
			The legend explains marks, so it appears when there are marks. A house that has done nothing all
			evening would otherwise open on five keys for four colours and a dotted line that are nowhere on
			the board — which is exactly the state a new owner sees first.
		*@
		<div class="board-foot">
			@if (_hasMarks)
			{
				<span class="board-key"><StateChip State="AreaState.AutoActive" Bare="true" Kelvin="2700" /><span class="board-swatch blk-lit" style="--blk: @KelvinColour.Css(2700)"></span> lit by the engine (colour = actual warmth)</span>
				<span class="board-key"><StateChip State="AreaState.OverriddenOn" Bare="true" /><span class="board-swatch blk-hand"></span> set by hand</span>
				<span class="board-key"><StateChip State="AreaState.PreOff" Bare="true" /><span class="board-swatch blk-dimming"></span> warning dim</span>
				<span class="board-key"><StateChip State="AreaState.SuppressedOff" Bare="true" /><span class="board-swatch blk-held"></span> off by hand</span>
				<span class="board-key"><span class="board-swatch board-swatch-next"></span> what happens next</span>
			}
			<span class="board-foot-end">Tap a room for its page</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	@if (AreaView.HiddenNote(AreaView.SwitchedOffCount(_rooms)) is { } hidden)
	{
		<p class="rooms-off">
			@hidden.Lead
			<a href="config?section=areas">@hidden.LinkText</a>.
		</p>
	}

	@*
		The log. The same reports the lanes are drawn from, in words, with the reading that decided each one —
		which is what somebody arriving with "why didn't that light come on" is actually here for.

		It is a SUMMARY, so it shows what the Activity page shows when you open it: the same default category set,
		read from ActivityView rather than restated here, which means the housekeeping stays off it. Twelve rows of
		"Rechecked the room · automatic lighting is off here" is a summary of nothing, and the
		twelve-row budget is spent after the filter rather than before it — twelve rows worth reading, not twelve
		of which nine are noise. The chips that switch the categories deliberately do not appear here: the filtering
		control belongs on the page the link at the foot already goes to.
	*@
	<div class="board-loghead">
		<h2 class="board-title">Activity &mdash; every decision, newest first</h2>
		<span class="board-since">@LogFoot</span>
	</div>

	@if (_entries.Count == 0)
	{
		<div class="empty">
			<strong>Nothing has happened yet.</strong>
			<p>
				A quiet house can sit here for a long time. Each room appears the first time it decides
				something &mdash; movement, a change of house mode, or the light outside crossing the level it
				counts as dark.
			</p>
		</div>
	}
	else if (_logRows.Count == 0)
	{
		@*
			Everything held is housekeeping — the state the filter makes possible and the one it must not let look
			like an empty log. Reports exist, they are simply not the kind this summary carries, and the way to
			them is one tap.
		*@
		<div class="empty">
			<strong>Nothing but background tasks so far.</strong>
			<p>
				@BackgroundOnlyLine Rechecks and start-up are kept off this summary, so it stays a summary &mdash;
				<a href="activity">Activity</a> shows them.
			</p>
		</div>
	}
	else
	{
		@* Denser than the Activity page's own rows: there the log IS the page, here it sits under a board and
		   twelve two-line rows would outweigh what they explain. Same rows, one line each. *@
		<ol class="log log-compact">
			@foreach (ActivityRow row in _logRows)
			{
				<li class="log-row family-@AreaView.Family(row.Snapshot.State)" @key="row.Sequence">
					<div class="log-when">
						<time class="log-time" datetime="@row.At.ToString("o", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
							  title="@Full(row.At)">@Clock(row.At)</time>
						@if (row.IsAboutTheHouse)
						{
							@* Not attributed to a room, because no room did it. Which rooms reported it is in the
							   hover, so the one fact the missing name took with it is still reachable. *@
							<span class="log-room log-room-house" title="@ActivityView.ReportedBy(row)">@HouseLabel(row)</span>
						}
						else if (row.Snapshot.AreaId is { Length: > 0 })
						{
							<a class="log-room log-room-link" href="@RoomHref(row.Snapshot.AreaId)">@row.Room</a>
						}
						else
						{
							<span class="log-room">@row.Room</span>
						}
					</div>
					<div class="log-body">
						<span class="log-what">@row.Line.What</span>
						@if (row.Line.Why is { Length: > 0 } why)
						{
							<span class="log-why">@why</span>
						}
					</div>
				</li>
			}
		</ol>

		<p class="log-foot"><a href="activity">All activity &rsaquo;</a></p>
	}
}

@code {
	private IReadOnlyList<AreaSnapshot> _snapshots = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<RoomView> _rooms = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> _entries = [];

	// The log summary, in two steps. _kept is what the Activity page's default categories leave — counted, because
	// the footer has to say how many reports the filter is holding back. _logRows is those reports collapsed into
	// rows and cut to the budget, which is therefore spent on rows somebody wants to read rather than on
	// housekeeping. The lanes above go on reading _entries: the board draws what every room did, filtered or not.
	private int _kept;
	private IReadOnlyList<ActivityRow> _logRows = [];

	private IReadOnlyList<BoardLane> _lanes = [];
	private bool _hasMarks;
	private IReadOnlyList<FloorGroup<BoardLane>> _groups = [];
	private HashSet<string> _quiet = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);
	private IReadOnlyList<AreaSnapshot> _exceptions = [];
	private IReadOnlyList<BandSegment> _band = [];
	private BoardWindow _window = BoardWindow.Around(DateTimeOffset.Now, BoardView.LookBack, BoardView.LookAhead);
	private IReadOnlyList<DateTimeOffset> _ticks = [];
	private double _nowPercent;
	private IReadOnlyList<TimePeriodConfig>? _periods;
	private DateTimeOffset? _periodsStamp;
	private MasterSwitchView? _masterSwitch;
	private HouseModeView? _houseMode;
	private IReadOnlyList<PersonView> _people = [];
	private string? _modeMessage;
	private string? _modeConfirmation;
	private DateTimeOffset? _modeConfirmationUntil;
	private DateTimeOffset _now = DateTimeOffset.Now;
	private IDisposable? _subscription;
	private IDisposable? _ticker;

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		_snapshots = Cache.Snapshots;
		ReadHouseState();
		ReadBoard();

		// Sampled rather than raw: a house waking up can push a burst of transitions, and redrawing the board
		// once per snapshot would be wasted work on a page nobody is reading that fast.
		_subscription = Cache.Changes
			.Sample(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(400))
			.SubscribeSafe(_ => InvokeAsync(() =>
			{
				_snapshots = Cache.Snapshots;
				ReadHouseState();
				ReadBoard();
				StateHasChanged();
			}), Logger);

		// The page's own clock. The now-line, the last block's right edge and "lights out in 18 s" are only
		// honest while they move, so the board is rebuilt once a second whether or not the engine has said
		// anything. The master switch, house mode and who's-home are re-read on the same tick so a change made
		// in Home Assistant shows without a reload.
		_ticker = Observable.Interval(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1))
			.SubscribeSafe(_ => InvokeAsync(() =>
			{
				_now = DateTimeOffset.Now;

				// The switch confirmation is transient: it says "switching…" for a few seconds, then clears itself so
				// the panel does not carry a stale acknowledgement once the mode has actually landed.
				if (_modeConfirmationUntil is { } until && _now > until)
				{
					_modeConfirmation = null;
					_modeConfirmationUntil = null;
				}

				ReadHouseState();
				ReadBoard();
				StateHasChanged();
			}), Logger);
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Re-reads the three read-only house-state projections. Ordered house-mode first so its readiness
	///     bookkeeping is not clobbered by the master switch's, exactly as the old Modes page ordered its reads.
	/// </summary>
	private void ReadHouseState()
	{
		_houseMode = Service.GetHouseMode();
		_masterSwitch = Service.GetMasterSwitch();
		_people = Service.GetPeople();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Rebuilds the whole board: the window, one lane per switched-on room, the quiet split, the tray and
	///     the schedule band.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     <para>
	///         Rebuilt on every tick rather than cached, because almost all of it moves: the window steps on the
	///         hour, the newest block's right edge is "now", and a countdown in the tray is wrong the moment it
	///         stops being recalculated. Seventeen rooms over five hundred reports is a few thousand comparisons,
	///         which is nothing beside the render it feeds.
	///     </para>
	///     <para>
	///         The log is read once per pass as a list, and this page deliberately keeps no read mark: it always
	///         redraws the newest reports rather than counting how many arrived since it last looked. So it never
	///         needs the entries and a sequence number together, and cannot suffer the torn read that pairing two
	///         locked reads would cause. Anything added here that <i>does</i> want to say "4 new" must switch to
	///         <c>ActivityLog.Read()</c>, which returns both under one lock, rather than calling
	///         <c>Entries</c> and <c>Newest</c> in turn.
	///     </para>
	///     <para>
	///         Which rooms get a lane is <see cref="AreaView.VisibleCards"/>'s decision, unchanged: the engine
	///         keeps observing and publishing switched-off rooms, and only the rendering declines them. The
	///         footer under the board carries their count with a way back.
	///     </para>
	/// </remarks>
	private void ReadBoard()
	{
		_rooms = Service.GetRooms();
		_window = BoardWindow.Around(_now, BoardView.LookBack, BoardView.LookAhead);
		_ticks = _window.Ticks;
		_nowPercent = Math.Clamp(_window.PercentAt(_now), 0, 100);
		_entries = Log.Entries;

		// The summary's own steps, in the order the Activity page applies them: the reports worth a row at all, then
		// the categories it opens with, then the collapse into rows, then the budget. Limited rather than collapsed
		// whole — the buffer holds five hundred reports and this runs once a second, and a dozen rows is all the
		// markup below can use.
		//
		// The sift is the summary's alone. The lanes further up are drawn from every report the engine published,
		// start-up included: that snapshot is where a lane's first block begins, and it is a state the board draws
		// rather than a sentence the record reads.
		IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> kept =
			ActivityView.InCategories(ActivityView.Shown(_entries), ActivityView.DefaultCategories);

		_kept = kept.Count;
		_logRows = ActivityView.Rows(kept, BoardView.LogPreview);

		IReadOnlyList<AreaSnapshot> visible = AreaView.VisibleCards(_snapshots, _rooms);

		Dictionary<string, List<ActivityEntry>> byRoom = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);
		foreach (ActivityEntry entry in _entries)
		{
			if (!byRoom.TryGetValue(KeyOf(entry.Snapshot), out List<ActivityEntry>? room))
				byRoom[KeyOf(entry.Snapshot)] = room = [];

			room.Add(entry);
		}

		List<BoardLane> lanes = new(visible.Count);
		foreach (AreaSnapshot snapshot in visible)
		{
			string key = KeyOf(snapshot);
			IReadOnlyList<ActivityEntry> history = byRoom.TryGetValue(key, out List<ActivityEntry>? found) ? found : [];

			lanes.Add(new BoardLane(
				key,
				snapshot.AreaName,
				snapshot.AreaId,
				snapshot,
				BoardView.Blocks(history, _window, _now),
				BoardView.NextMark(snapshot, _window, _now)));
		}

		_lanes = lanes;
		_hasMarks = lanes.Any(lane => lane.Blocks.Count > 0 || lane.Next is not null);
		_exceptions = BoardView.Exceptions(visible);

		(IReadOnlyList<BoardLane> _, IReadOnlyList<BoardLane> quiet) = BoardView.Partition(lanes);
		_quiet = new HashSet<string>(quiet.Select(lane => lane.Key), StringComparer.Ordinal);

		// A registry that has not connected throws rather than answering. One unnamed group is the floorless
		// degradation FloorGrouping already specifies, so the failure mode is a flat board — the lanes are what
		// somebody came here for, and refusing to draw them because floors were unavailable would trade the
		// whole page for a decoration.
		try
		{
			_groups = FloorGrouping.Group(lanes, lane => lane.AreaId, Catalog.AreaRegistry);
		}
		catch (InvalidOperationException)
		{
			_groups = lanes.Count == 0 ? [] : [new FloorGroup<BoardLane>(null, lanes)];
		}

		ReadSchedule();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Re-reads the circadian table for the band above the lanes.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Cached against the store file's last-write time, so the per-second ticker costs a stat rather than a
	///     YAML parse — the same bargain <see cref="ModeService"/> already makes with the same file. A parse
	///     failure keeps the last good table instead of blanking the band mid-save, and a house whose sun times
	///     Home Assistant cannot answer for simply loses its sun-anchored boundaries, exactly as the engine does.
	/// </remarks>
	private void ReadSchedule()
	{
		if (Engine.Store.Exists)
		{
			DateTimeOffset? stamp = Engine.Store.LastWrittenUtc;

			if (_periods is null || stamp != _periodsStamp)
			{
				try
				{
					_periods = Engine.Store.Load().Periods;
					_periodsStamp = stamp;
				}
				catch (LightingConfigException exception)
				{
					Logger.LogDebug(exception, "Could not read the schedule for the board; keeping the last good copy.");
				}
			}
		}

		SunTimes sun;
		try
		{
			(TimeOnly? sunrise, TimeOnly? sunset) = Catalog.SunTimesToday();
			sun = new SunTimes(sunrise, sunset);
		}
		catch (InvalidOperationException)
		{
			sun = SunTimes.Unknown;
		}

		_band = BoardView.Band(_periods ?? [], sun, _window);
	}

	/// <summary>What a report is filed under, matching the snapshot cache's own key so the two agree.</summary>
	private static string KeyOf(AreaSnapshot snapshot) =>
		snapshot.AreaId is { Length: > 0 } areaId ? areaId : snapshot.AreaName;

	/// <summary>
	///     Where a room's own page is. The id is escaped because it reaches the URL, and a room configured with
	///     explicit entities has no area id and so no page — <c>null</c> makes Blazor drop the attribute, which
	///     leaves an anchor that is neither a link nor a tab stop rather than one pointing at nowhere.
	/// </summary>
	private static string? RoomHref(string? areaId) =>
		areaId is { Length: > 0 } id ? $"room/{Uri.EscapeDataString(id)}" : null;

	/// <summary>A lane's hover explanation: the state in the words every other surface uses.</summary>
	private static string LaneTitle(BoardLane lane) => $"{lane.Name} — {StateGlyph.For(lane.Latest.State).Word}";

	/// <summary>
	///     Which edge a tray chip carries. The warning dim is the one exception that is about to act, so it is
	///     the one that gets the warning colour; the other three are all somebody's decision standing, and the
	///     violet says so — the same split the state chips make.
	/// </summary>
	private static string TrayEdge(AreaState state) => state == AreaState.PreOff ? "tray-warn" : "tray-human";

	/// <summary>
	///     Where the now-line stands: past the sticky name column, then the fraction of the track that has
	///     already happened. The column's width is a token so the phone override moves the line with it.
	/// </summary>
	private string NowOffset => $"calc(var(--lane-name) + (100% - var(--lane-name)) * {Pct(_nowPercent / 100.0)})";

	/// <summary>
	///     The board's honest label about how far back it can see: a restart wipes the morning, and a reader who
	///     does not know that will read an empty lane as a room that did nothing.
	/// </summary>
	private string SinceLine =>
		Engine.LastStartedUtc is { } started
			? $"since adaptive lighting started, {BoardView.Clock(started)}"
			: "since adaptive lighting started";

	/// <summary>What the log below is showing, what the filter is holding back, and the cap it is all held to.</summary>
	private string LogFoot => BoardView.LogFoot(_entries.Count, _kept, _logRows.Count, ActivityLog.Capacity);

	/// <summary>
	///     The count in front of the housekeeping-only empty state, so it says how much is behind the link rather
	///     than merely that something is.
	/// </summary>
	private string BackgroundOnlyLine => _entries.Count == 1
		? "The one report so far is a background task."
		: $"All {_entries.Count} reports so far are background tasks.";

	/// <summary>
	///     What stands where a room name would: the house, and how many rooms a collapsed row came from.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     The count is shown rather than left to the hover because it is the answer to the question the stack of
	///     identical rows used to raise — "did that really happen six times?" — and a reader who never hovers is
	///     exactly the reader who was misled by it.
	/// </remarks>
	private static string HouseLabel(ActivityRow row) =>
		row.Rooms.Count > 1 ? $"House · {row.Rooms.Count} rooms" : "House";

	/// <summary>The first tick hangs off the left edge and the last off the right, so neither is clipped.</summary>
	private string? TickClass(int index) =>
		index == 0 ? "board-tick-first" : index == _window.Ticks.Count - 1 ? "board-tick-last" : null;

	/// <summary>
	///     A number bound for a CSS length.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Invariant, always. A Norwegian host formats 62.5 as "62,5", and <c>left: 62,5%</c> is not a length —
	///     the declaration is dropped and every block on the board stacks at the left edge.
	/// </remarks>
	private static string Pct(double value) => value.ToString("0.###", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

	private static string Clock(DateTimeOffset at) =>
		at.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

	private static string Full(DateTimeOffset at) =>
		at.ToLocalTime().ToString("dddd d MMMM yyyy, HH:mm:ss", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

	/// <summary>
	///     The first-run state's opening line. Says what set-up did and what follows from it, in that order: the
	///     rooms were found (the system's half is done) and nothing will change (so nothing is broken).
	/// </summary>
	private string FoundLine => _rooms.Count == 1
		? "Setting up found one room. It isn't switched on yet, so no lights will change."
		: $"Setting up found {_rooms.Count} rooms. None are switched on yet, so no lights will change.";

	private static string LightsWord(int lights) => lights == 1 ? "1 light" : $"{lights} lights";

	/// <summary>Flips the master switch through the existing config-resolved toggle, then re-reads the house state.</summary>
	private void ToggleMaster()
	{
		if (_masterSwitch is { IsAvailable: true, Toggle.CanToggle: true } master)
			Service.Toggle(master.Toggle);

		ReadHouseState();
	}

	/// <summary>
	///     Switches the house mode through the existing config-resolved <see cref="ModeService.SelectHouseMode"/>,
	///     then re-reads. A service call is fire-and-forget, so the highlight must reflect what Home Assistant
	///     actually reports, not what was asked for.
	/// </summary>
	private void SelectMode(string option)
	{
		// A service call is fire-and-forget, so this is a "requested" acknowledgement, not "done": the current-mode
		// highlight below still comes from what Home Assistant reports on the next read, not from what was asked.
		if (Service.SelectHouseMode(option))
		{
			_modeMessage = null;
			_modeConfirmation = $"Switching to {option}…";
			_modeConfirmationUntil = DateTimeOffset.Now.AddSeconds(6);
		}
		else
		{
			_modeConfirmation = null;
			_modeConfirmationUntil = null;
			_modeMessage = $"Couldn't switch to '{option}' — Home Assistant may be offline.";
		}

		ReadHouseState();
	}

	private static string MasterClass(MasterSwitchView master) =>
		!master.IsAvailable ? "unknown" : master.AdaptiveLightingOn ? "on" : "off";

	private static string MasterStateWord(MasterSwitchView master) =>
		!master.IsAvailable ? "unknown" : master.AdaptiveLightingOn ? "On" : "Off";

	/// <summary>
	///     The line under the master switch, or <c>null</c> while there is nothing to say.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Only news earns a line. "Lights adjust automatically" restates the word beside it and the colour on its
	///     edge, and a permanent sentence saying nothing is wrong is precisely the reassurance this design refuses
	///     to buy with the board's own space; it lives in the button's hover instead. The other two states keep
	///     theirs, because paused and unreachable are the two a reader has to be told about — and paused is the one
	///     place the control's real consequence can be spelled out.
	/// </remarks>
	private static string? MasterNote(MasterSwitchView master)
	{
		if (!master.IsAvailable)
		{
			return master.IsReady
				? "Home Assistant doesn't know the master switch, so its state can't be shown."
				: "Waiting for Home Assistant — state unknown.";
		}

		return master.AdaptiveLightingOn
			? null
			: "Paused — nothing was turned off, but no lights will change until you turn it back on.";
	}

	private static string MasterTitle(MasterSwitchView master)
	{
		if (!master.IsAvailable)
		{
			return master.IsReady
				? "Home Assistant doesn't know this switch."
				: "Waiting for Home Assistant.";
		}

		return master.Toggle.CanToggle
			? $"Turn adaptive lighting {(master.AdaptiveLightingOn ? "off" : "on")}"
			: "This entity can't be switched on or off.";
	}

	/// <inheritdoc/>
	public void Dispose()
	{
		_subscription?.Dispose();
		_ticker?.Dispose();
	}
}
